<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willem Adriaan van der Stel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1664">1664</a>–<a href="/years/1733">1733</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Willem Adriaan van der Stel succeeded his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-van-der-stel">Simon van der Stel</a> as governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, serving from 1699 to 1707. He and other officials used their positions to build up large private farms, notably his estate of Vergelegen, and to dominate the colony's markets. Free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burgher</a> settlers protested to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, which dismissed him in 1707.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willem-adriaan-van-der-stel.jpg" alt="Willem Adriaan van der Stel" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Weenix · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Simon_van_der_Stel_and_his_son_Willem_Adriaan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Willem Adriaan van der Stel was born on 24 August 1664, the eldest of six children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-van-der-stel">Simon van der Stel</a> and Johanna Jacoba Six, prominent members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchant class, and was baptised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>. His paternal grandfather had been the commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC). At fifteen, in 1679, he went with his father to the Cape, where he worked for the company as a bookkeeper. In 1684 he married Maria de Haze and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, where he became a schepen in 1691 and held the lordship of Nieuw and Oud-Vossemeer on Tholen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Appointed to succeed his father, he returned to the Cape in January 1699. He took a keen interest in horticulture and agriculture, conducted extensive farming experiments, sent aloes to the Hortus Botanicus Amsterdam and wrote one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s earliest gardening almanacs. He expanded the company's gardens, sent expeditions north into the interior, established the Land van Waveren, now Tulbagh, and laid the cornerstone of the Groote Kerk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>.</p><p>Vergelegen was granted to him in 1700, and he spent much of the company's resources on it. His decisions on who could take part in the monopoly of wine and meat turned the free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> against him. In 1706 Adam Tas, Willem van Zijl and Henning Husing drew up a petition, signed by some 63 of the 550 burghers and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. It was at first rejected, and Van der Stel had Tas imprisoned in the Black Hole of the Castle of Good Hope. Because 31 signatories were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> was at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the company feared some might spy for the French, and on 23 April 1707 it dismissed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He left the Cape in 1708 and spent the rest of his life in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>; he died on 11 November 1733. Afterwards no company employee was allowed to own land in the colony, and Vergelegen was sold, divided into four farms and its homestead ordered demolished. Most accounts call his rule authoritarian, marked by favouritism and the misuse of company assets, though some argue this was not unique to him, and others point to his plans in agriculture and his part in the development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-dutch">Cape Dutch</a> architecture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Willem Adriaan van der Stel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colony was run by Company officials, including Simon van der Stel and his son <b>Willem Adriaan</b>, and its economy depended on wine, wheat and enslaved labour brought from the East Indies, India and Madagascar.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-cape-colony">Dutch Cape Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.38,4.64&amp;year=1708" aria-label="Haarlem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4654 356 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Willem Adriaan van der Stel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5104 626h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5104 626h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.38,4.64&amp;year=1708">What was here in 1708? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
